## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to diagnose production rollouts with service meshes and canaries, focusing on end-to-end validation, live traffic checks, and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Canary/rollout health in Istio
- Rollout status and events
- Readiness/Liveness probes relevance
- Safe rollback without downtime
- Synthetic traffic validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal helper to parse rollout JSON and extract canary status (pseudo, for illustration)
function getCanaryStatus(rolloutJson) {
  try {
    const r = JSON.parse(rolloutJson);
    return r?.status?.canary?.traffic || 0;
  } catch {
    return 0;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback decision with a health-check gate?
- What metrics would you collect to prove P95 stability during rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of idempotency, distributed transactions, and fault tolerance in real-time payment systems. It mirrors fintech patterns at Plaid and Lyft, where user actions must not duplicate or lose funds.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys
- Outbox pattern and sagas
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once
- Dead-letter queues and backoff strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch: idempotency check and outbox write
function process(tx, store) {
  const key = tx.id;
  if (store.exists(key)) return;
  store.saveOutbox(tx);
  // publish events ...
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency guarantees under partial failures?
- How do you monitor for orphaned or duplicate transactions in production?
